-- Q18 [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub18$Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia$sub18$, $qt18$A pharmaceutical team develops a small, water-soluble compound that, when injected into a patient's bloodstream, circulates freely throughout the body but fails to reach neurons in the brain and spinal cord. The team concludes the drug is being kept out of the central nervous system by a barrier formed at the CNS capillary wall. Which glial cell's barrier-forming activity most directly accounts for the drug's exclusion?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "Astrocytes, because they form the blood-brain barrier that restricts which substances pass from circulating blood into the CNS"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Microglia, because they phagocytose the drug from the extracellular space before it can reach the neurons"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ependymal cells, because they pump the drug out of the cerebrospinal fluid back into the circulating blood"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oligodendrocytes, because their myelin sheaths wrap the neurons and seal them off from the drug"}]$op18$::jsonb, 'A', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is A because astrocytes form the blood-brain barrier (BBB), and that barrier is what restricts which blood-borne substances can enter the CNS. The textbook states that astrocytes "make contact with both capillaries and neurons in the CNS" and "also form the blood-brain barrier—a structure that blocks entrance of toxic substances into the brain," while the BBB itself is described as "a physiological barrier that keeps many substances that circulate in the rest of the body from getting into the central nervous system." The same passage notes that because nutrient molecules such as glucose and amino acids can cross but most others cannot, the BBB "causes problems with drug delivery to the CNS," which is exactly the scenario described. The drug's exclusion therefore reflects astrocyte-supported barrier function at the vessel wall, not clearance, transport out of CSF, or insulation of the neuron. (Choice A) Correct: astrocytes contribute to the blood-brain barrier, and that barrier is the structure that determines which substances cross from blood into the CNS, accounting for the drug's exclusion. (Choice B) Incorrect: microglia perform immune surveillance and phagocytosis of dead cells and pathogens, but this clearance role does not constitute the blood-to-CNS barrier that blocks the drug from entering in the first place. (Choice C) Incorrect: ependymal cells filter blood to make cerebrospinal fluid rather than pumping circulating drugs out of the CSF, so they do not produce the vessel-wall barrier responsible here. (Choice D) Incorrect: oligodendrocytes form myelin sheaths that insulate CNS axons for faster impulse conduction; myelin is an electrical insulator around axons, not a barrier separating blood from the CNS. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting a drug-delivery scenario and inferring which specific glial cell's barrier function explains the observed exclusion of a blood-borne compound from the CNS.$ex18$, 'medium', '3A', $cc18$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
