UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a18C$Invokes ependymal CSF handling and reverse transport instead of the BBB at the capillary wall$a18C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a18D$Treats oligodendrocyte myelin (an axonal electrical insulator) as a blood-to-CNS diffusion barrier$a18D$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub19$Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia$sub19$, $qt19$During an ischemic stroke, a cluster of neurons in the affected region fires repeatedly, and extracellular K+ accumulates to abnormally high levels in the surrounding fluid. A researcher tracking the recovery of the local ionic environment that sets the resting membrane potential wants to identify the glial cell normally responsible for clearing this excess K+. That cell is:$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "Microglia, because they engulf and digest the excess potassium ions the way they remove cellular debris in damaged tissue."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Astrocytes, because they clear excess extracellular K+ and maintain the concentration of ions in the extracellular fluid."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ependymal cells, because they secrete the excess potassium into the cerebrospinal fluid that they continually produce."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oligodendrocytes, because the myelin they wrap around the axon reabsorbs the potassium leaking out of it."}]$op19$::jsonb, 'B', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is B because astrocytes are the CNS glia equipped to take up excess extracellular K+ and to maintain the ionic concentrations in the extracellular fluid that establish the membrane potential. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that "Following a stroke or other ischemic event, extracellular K+ levels are elevated. The astrocytes in the area are equipped to clear excess K+ to aid the pump," and that "Glial cells, especially astrocytes, are responsible for maintaining the chemical environment of the CNS tissue. The concentrations of ions in the extracellular fluid are the basis for how the membrane potential is established." OpenStax Biology 2e likewise notes that astrocytes "regulate the concentrations of ions and chemicals in the extracellular fluid." Because the membrane potential depends on the extracellular K+ concentration, the astrocyte's K+ buffering is exactly the function needed to restore the ionic environment after repeated firing. (Choice A) Microglia "ingest and digest" diseased or damaged cells and pathogens as CNS-resident macrophages, a phagocytic role, not an ion-buffering one. (Choice B) is correct because astrocytes clear excess extracellular K+ and maintain extracellular ion concentrations. (Choice C) Ependymal cells "filter blood to make cerebrospinal fluid," a secretory/CSF-production role distinct from directly buffering K+ in the extracellular fluid around neurons. (Choice D) Oligodendrocytes "insulate axons in the CNS" with myelin; myelin insulates the axon and does not absorb or buffer extracellular ions. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because the student must connect the ischemic scenario and the rise in extracellular K+ to the specific glial function that restores the ionic basis of the membrane potential, rather than simply recalling a definition.$ex19$, 'medium', '3A', $cc19$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a19A$Assigns ion buffering to the phagocytic immune glia (microglia) by treating ions as debris to be digested$a19A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a19C$Substitutes the ependymal cell's CSF-production step for direct extracellular ion buffering$a19C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a19D$Treats the insulating function of oligodendrocyte myelin as if it absorbed extracellular ions$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub20$Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia$sub20$, $qt20$An injury to a patient's central nervous system selectively destroys a single glial cell type, after which the fluid that normally floats and cushions the brain is no longer produced and its mechanical shock-absorbing protection is no longer replenished. Which glial population was MOST likely lost, and why does this explain the failure?$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "Astrocytes were lost, so the cushion fails because the extracellular ion environment can no longer be maintained around neurons."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Microglia were lost, so the cushion fails because dead cellular debris is no longer scavenged and degraded in the tissue."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oligodendrocytes were lost, so the cushion fails because central axons are no longer insulated by their myelin sheaths."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ependymal cells were lost, so the cushion fails because the cerebrospinal fluid that floats and shock-absorbs the brain is no longer produced."}]$op20$::jsonb, 'D', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is D because among the four central nervous system glia, only ependymal cells are involved in producing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), and CSF is precisely the fluid that floats and cushions the brain. OpenStax Biology 2e states that ependymal cells \"are involved in the production of cerebrospinal fluid, which serves as a cushion for the brain,\" and separately that the brain \"floats in CSF, which acts as a cushion and shock absorber.\" If the cell that makes the cushioning fluid is destroyed, the shock-absorbing protection cannot be replenished, so the lost population must be the ependymal cells. The other three CNS glia each have a real but distinct role that does not generate the CSF cushion. (Choice A) is a partial truth: astrocytes genuinely \"regulate the concentrations of ions and chemicals in the extracellular fluid,\" but ion buffering supports neuronal signaling, not the production of the cushioning fluid. (Choice B) is a partial truth: microglia truly \"scavenge and degrade dead cells,\" yet waste clearance is not the source of CSF mechanical cushioning. (Choice C) is a tempting reversal of roles: oligodendrocytes do \"form myelin sheaths around axons in the CNS,\" but myelination insulates axons and is unrelated to making cushioning fluid. (Choice D) is correct because ependymal cells are the CNS glia that produce the CSF that cushions and shock-absorbs the brain. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the observed loss of a functional outcome (no CSF cushioning) back to the specific glial cell that produces that fluid while ruling out three other glia whose real functions do not generate the cushion.$ex20$, 'hard', '3A', $cc20$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a20A$Real support glia with a real function (extracellular ion buffering) that is not the source of the CSF cushion$a20A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a20B$True microglial function (phagocytosis of dead cells) substituted for the source of CSF cushioning$a20B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a20C$510-tempting myelination role attributed to fluid cushioning; correct glial function, wrong outcome linkage$a20C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [CNS Tissue Organization | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub21$CNS Tissue Organization$sub21$, $qt21$A neuropathologist examines a fresh, unstained coronal section of a human brain and notes a deep region that appears glossy and pale white, in contrast to the darker outer cortex. Microscopically, this pale region is composed predominantly of:$qt21$, $op21$[{"label": "A", "text": "Myelinated axons, whose lipid-rich insulating sheaths give the tissue its pale, fatty white color"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Unmyelinated neuron cell bodies and their dendrites, which give the tissue its characteristic color"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Interneurons together with the surrounding neuropil of synaptic connections between cells"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Glial cell bodies that filter the blood to produce cerebrospinal fluid for the tissue"}]$op21$::jsonb, 'A', $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is A because white matter is white precisely because its axons are wrapped in a lipid-rich substance called myelin, and lipids appear as white, fatty material. The OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology text states that white matter comprises "the regions with many axons" and that "white matter is white because axons are insulated by a lipid-rich substance called myelin"; OpenStax Biology similarly notes that "myelinated axons make up the 'white matter.'" The pale, glossy region deep to the darker cortex is therefore the myelin-laden axon tracts, not cell-body-rich tissue. (Choice A) Correct: myelinated axons predominate, and their lipid-rich myelin produces the pale white appearance. (Choice B) Incorrect: cell bodies and dendrites characterize gray matter, the opposite tissue type ("the regions with many cell bodies and dendrites"), not white matter. (Choice C) Incorrect: interneurons and synaptic neuropil are features of gray matter, where cell bodies and synaptic processing concentrate, rather than of the axon-dominated white matter. (Choice D) Incorrect: the glial cells that filter blood to make cerebrospinal fluid are ependymal cells lining the ventricles, a distinct function unrelated to what composes white matter. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires direct recall of the established structural composition of CNS white matter and the source of its color.$ex21$, 'easy', '3A', $cc21$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 70
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a21B$Attributing gray matter's cell-body/dendrite composition to white matter (the opposite tissue type)$a21B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a21C$Citing interneurons and synaptic neuropil, which are true features of gray matter, as the makeup of white matter$a21C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a21D$Conflating a real glial function (ependymal cells producing CSF) with the structural composition of white matter$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [CNS Tissue Organization | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub22$CNS Tissue Organization$sub22$, $qt22$A pathologist cuts a frontal section through a freshly removed, unstained human brain and notes that the outer cortical band appears distinctly darker than the deeper tissue it borders. Asked to predict what this darker region contains, the pathologist should conclude that it is dominated by:$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "long myelinated axons coursing in tracts between distant brain regions"}, {"label": "B", "text": "neuron cell bodies and dendrites, which lack the myelin that whitens neighboring tissue"}, {"label": "C", "text": "lipid-rich myelin bundled into the insulated fiber tracts that color the tissue"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Schwann-cell–myelinated fibers exiting the central nervous system toward the periphery"}]$op22$::jsonb, 'B', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is B because the darker (gray) region of an unstained CNS section corresponds to gray matter, defined as the regions containing many neuron cell bodies and dendrites, which are not coated by the lipid-rich myelin that makes neighboring white matter pale. OpenStax A&P 2e states that the two regions of nervous tissue "are often referred to as gray matter (the regions with many cell bodies and dendrites) or white matter (the regions with many axons)," and that "white matter is white because axons are insulated by a lipid-rich substance called myelin," whereas next to that white matter the gray matter "is just darker—hence, gray." OpenStax Biology 2e reinforces that "neuron and glial cell bodies make up the 'gray matter,'" while "myelinated axons make up the 'white matter.'" Because a frontal brain section is an unstained CNS specimen, the darker cortex must be cell-body–rich, unmyelinated tissue. (Choice A) Long myelinated axons in tracts are the defining content of white matter, the paler tissue, not the darker gray cortex. (Choice B) Correct: the darker region is gray matter, dominated by neuron cell bodies and dendrites that lack myelin. (Choice C) Lipid-rich myelin in insulated tracts is exactly what whitens white matter, so it cannot explain the darker appearance of the gray region. (Choice D) Schwann cells myelinate axons only in the peripheral nervous system, so Schwann-cell–myelinated fibers are not the constituents of a central cortical region. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring unobserved cellular composition from a gross visual feature by applying the structural definition of gray versus white matter.$ex22$, 'medium', '3A', $cc22$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a22A$Assigning the defining feature of white matter (myelinated axon tracts) to the darker gray region$a22A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a22C$Believing myelin darkens tissue when in fact lipid-rich myelin whitens it$a22C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a22D$Invoking Schwann-cell (PNS) myelination for a CNS structure$a22D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [CNS Tissue Organization | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub23$CNS Tissue Organization$sub23$, $qt23$A neuroanatomist examines a stained section of the brainstem and identifies a discrete, localized cluster of neuron cell bodies that together serve as a center for a single coordinated function within the central nervous system. Applying standard anatomical terminology, this structure is correctly designated a:$qt23$, $op23$[{"label": "A", "text": "ganglion"}, {"label": "B", "text": "tract"}, {"label": "C", "text": "nucleus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "nerve"}]$op23$::jsonb, 'C', $ex23$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is C because a localized collection of neuron cell bodies located within the central nervous system is, by anatomical convention, termed a nucleus. The naming of clusters of neuron cell bodies depends on location: the text states that \"A localized collection of neuron cell bodies in the CNS is referred to as a nucleus. In the PNS, a cluster of neuron cell bodies is referred to as a ganglion,\" and the glossary defines a nucleus in the nervous system as \"a localized collection of neuron cell bodies that are functionally related; a 'center' of neural function.\" Because the described structure is inside the brainstem (CNS) and consists of cell bodies acting as a functional center, the cell-body terms apply rather than the axon-bundle terms, and the CNS variant (nucleus) is selected over the PNS variant (ganglion). (Choice A) A ganglion is the term for a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS, not the CNS, so it names cell bodies but in the wrong nervous-system division. (Choice B) A tract is a bundle of axons in the CNS, so although it is the correct division, it names fibers rather than cell bodies. (Choice C) A nucleus is the correct CNS term for a localized cluster of neuron cell bodies serving as a center of function, matching both the location and the structure described. (Choice D) A nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS, mismatching both the CNS location and the cell-body structure described. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires mapping a described structure to the correct term by jointly resolving two variables, anatomical location (CNS vs. PNS) and structural content (cell bodies vs. axons), rather than recalling a single isolated definition.$ex23$, 'medium', '3A', $cc23$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc23$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a23A$correct cell-body term but for the PNS division instead of the CNS$a23A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a23B$correct CNS division but names an axon bundle rather than a cluster of cell bodies$a23B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a23D$conflates cell bodies with axons and CNS with PNS (PNS axon-bundle term)$a23D$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [CNS Tissue Organization | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub24$CNS Tissue Organization$sub24$, $qt24$A neuroanatomist traces a single bundle of retinal ganglion-cell axons from the eye toward the brain. She labels the bundle 'optic nerve' where it lies in the periphery, then follows it past the optic chiasm to the point where the identical axons now run entirely within the cranium. Naming the bundle by its central-nervous-system designation and classifying the tissue it represents, she should record it as the:$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "optic nucleus, a CNS bundle of axons classified as white matter"}, {"label": "B", "text": "optic nerve, the same bundle now reclassified as gray matter once central"}, {"label": "C", "text": "optic ganglion, a CNS cluster of cell bodies forming gray matter"}, {"label": "D", "text": "optic tract, a CNS bundle of myelinated axons forming white matter"}]$op24$::jsonb, 'D', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is D because a bundle of axons inside the CNS is a tract, and because tracts are composed of myelinated axons they constitute white matter. The text states that "A bundle of axons, or fibers, found in the CNS is called a tract whereas the same thing in the PNS would be called a nerve," and that the retinal axons "are called the optic nerve as they leave the eye, but when they are inside the cranium, they are referred to as the optic tract." Table 12.1 pairs the "Bundle of Axons (i.e., white matter)" with the term Tract in the CNS, and the white appearance arises because "axons are insulated by a lipid-rich substance called myelin." The naming change occurs at the optic chiasm, the border between peripheral and central, even though the axons are physically the same. (Choice A) The optic nucleus is incorrect because a nucleus is a CNS collection of neuron cell bodies (gray matter), not a bundle of axons, so the "bundle of axons / white matter" pairing is mismatched to the wrong CNS term. (Choice B) Keeping the name optic nerve is incorrect because "nerve" is the PNS term and applies only in the periphery; moreover an axon bundle is white matter, not gray matter, so both the name and the tissue class are wrong. (Choice C) The optic ganglion is incorrect because a ganglion is a PNS cluster of cell bodies, and although cell-body clusters are indeed gray matter, the structure in question is an axon bundle, not a collection of cell bodies. (Choice D) The optic tract correctly applies the CNS term for an axon bundle and correctly classifies that myelinated bundle as white matter. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the CNS-versus-PNS tract/nerve distinction with the white-matter (myelinated-axon) concept to predict both the correct central term and its matching tissue category for a single relabeled bundle.$ex24$, 'hard', '3A', $cc24$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a24A$Correct tissue class (white matter) but swaps in the CNS cell-body term (nucleus) for the CNS axon-bundle term (tract)$a24A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a24B$Keeps the PNS name centrally and inverts the tissue class, treating an axon bundle as gray matter$a24B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a24C$Correctly links cell-body clusters to gray matter but applies the PNS cell-body term and the wrong structure type to an axon bundle$a24C$ FROM q;

-- Q25 [CNS Tissue Organization | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub25$CNS Tissue Organization$sub25$, $qt25$A spinal-cord cross-section appears as a gray butterfly-shaped region nested inside a surrounding white oval, giving the unstained tissue its characteristic two-toned contrast. A patient develops a demyelinating disease that progressively destroys the myelin sheaths wrapping the cord's axons. Which region's defining gross appearance is most directly degraded as the disease advances?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "The white matter, whose pale appearance comes from the myelin sheathing its axons, would lose the feature that defines it"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The gray matter, whose color comes from the myelinated interneurons packed within it, would lose its defining appearance"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The gray matter, because its dense cell bodies depend on intact myelin to remain dark, would be most affected"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both regions would be unaffected because their colors come from blood content rather than from myelin"}]$op25$::jsonb, 'A', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure'. The answer is A because the whiteness of white matter is produced directly by the lipid-rich myelin that insulates its axons, so destroying that myelin removes the very feature responsible for the region's pale appearance. In a spinal-cord cross-section, the white oval is made of myelinated axons while the gray butterfly is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and interneurons, so only the myelin-dependent white matter loses its defining color when myelin is destroyed (Bio 2e: "Myelinated axons make up the 'white matter' and neuron and glial cell bodies make up the 'gray matter'"). The A&P text states that "white matter is white because axons are insulated by a lipid-rich substance called myelin," whereas gray matter's color comes from blood content or simply from being darker by contrast, not from myelin. Therefore the disease most directly fades the white-versus-gray contrast by attacking the white region's source of whiteness. (Choice A) Correct: white matter's pale color is a direct consequence of axonal myelin, so demyelination erases its defining appearance. (Choice B) Incorrect: gray matter's color comes from cell bodies and interneurons, not from myelin, and the interneurons there are not the source of any "white" coloration. (Choice C) Incorrect: this reverses the relationship; gray matter appears dark by contrast and from blood content, and its cell bodies do not depend on myelin to stay dark. (Choice D) Incorrect: although blood content can tint gray matter pinkish, the whiteness of white matter is specifically due to myelin, so myelin loss does change appearance. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the composition of white matter (myelinated axons) to the physical cause of its whiteness (lipid-rich myelin) and then predicting how a demyelinating insult alters the tissue's gross appearance.$ex25$, 'hard', '3A', $cc25$Neuron & Glial Cell Structure$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
